// A user declaration shadows the prelude only WITHIN ITS OWN KIND.
//
// `Allocator` is a prelude STRUCT. Declaring a user ENUM of that name does not
// remove it, so the name still resolves to the struct - the reference looks
// structs up before enums - and matching on it is refused for not being an enum.
//
// The port briefly did the opposite, twice over: a shadow set that ignored the
// kind removed the prelude struct outright, and a resolution order that asked
// enums first bound the name to the user's enum. Both accepted this program.
// Worse, they accepted a program that never mentions `match` at all: with only
// `fn f(x: *const Allocator)` both compilers compiled cleanly and the parameter
// meant two different types, which is the face a user meets rather than reports.
enum Allocator { A | B }
fn pick(e: *const Allocator) -> int { return match *e { Allocator::A => 1 | Allocator::B => 2 }; }
fn main() { println(pick(&Allocator::A)); }
